  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...As of 2:10 AM EDT...Current GOES 16 WV imagery shows \nseveral upper impulses tracking over the Great Lakes around the \nperiphery of a closed upper low located near James Bay. This upper \nlow is vertically stacked with an occluded sfc low, with the \ntrailing cold front extending through the eastern Great Lakes. Some\nadditional isolated showers may be possible through today due \nto westerly flow over the Great Lakes, cool temperatures aloft, \nand a couple of additional upper shortwaves tracking through the\nmean flow. Best chance will be for the southern ADKs and \nsouthern Greens where westerly upslope will locally enhance \nlift. It will also become quite breezy behind the cold front \nthis morning. Tight pressure gradient and deep mixing within the\nlow-level cold advection regime will allow for gusts up to \n20-30 kt, strongest where flow is channeled down the Mohawk \nValley, through the Capital District, and into the Berkshires. \nWe accordingly bumped up winds quite a bit from the NBM in \ncoordination with neighboring WFOs. \n\nShower chances generally diminish after sunset this evening, \nalthough some additional lake/upslope enhanced showers may redevelop \ntomorrow over the ADKs/southern Greens as we will still be close to \nthe upper trough and associated cold pool aloft. Any showers should \nbe light, with less coverage compared to this afternoon. Tomorrow \nwill still see breezy W/NW winds, albeit not as strong as this \nafternoon. With sfc high pressure building overhead Friday night, \nfavorable radiational cooling conditions will likely allow temps to \ndrop into the 30s to low 40s for most areas. Some patchy frost will \nbe possible in the high terrain areas, although the growing season \nhas already ended there. The sfc high continues to strengthen \nthrough the first half of the weekend beneath an amplifying \nupper ridge, so we should see large-scale subsidence leading to \ncontinued tranquil weather. Saturday looks like a beautiful fall\nday with light winds and highs in the 60s.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...By the second half of the weekend, the remnants of \nwhat is currently Hurricane Isaias in the Gulf will track towards \nour region. There remains some uncertainty in the track of this \nsystem as it approaches our area, especially with regards to the\nnorthern extent of the precip shield given that it will be \nrunning into upper ridging and the strong sfc high to our east. \nThere are plenty of deterministic and ensemble members that have\nour entire area receiving rain, although the probability for\nappreciable rain is highest south of I-90. Given very dry \nantecedent conditions (southern areas will be going on close to \n2 weeks without appreciable rain), any rain that falls will be \nvery beneficial. NBM probabilities for >1\" continue to range \nfrom <10% across our northern zones to around 30% near I-84, \nwith probabilities for >2\" under 5% area-wide. Therefore, not \nexpecting any flooding issues with this rain despite its \ntropical origins. Showers should come to an end from W/NW to \nE/SE through the day Monday. A strong cold front then looks to \ntrack through the region sometime TUE/WED next week, bringing \nadditional chances for showers and another shot of cool air to \nthe region.",
